From: Grische <github@grische.xyz>
Date: Thu, 22 Oct 2026 19:30:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] mmc: core: Skip the SDIO voltage switch when already at 1.8V

The NanoPi R2S Plus has an RTL8822CS SDIO Wi-Fi module on the sdmmc_ext
controller of the RK3328. The I/O supply of the slot is a fixed 1.8V
regulator. So the host already signals at 1.8V when it powers up the
card. The host is described with sd-uhs-sdr104.

Per SDIO Simplified Specification Version 3.00, section 3.1.2, a card
that already runs at 1.8V signaling sets S18A to 0 in the R4 response.
The RTL8822CS sets S18A to 1 anyway. The core then sends CMD11 to start
the voltage switch. Per Physical Layer Simplified Specification Version
3.01, section 4.2.4.4, a card whose signaling level is already 1.8V
treats CMD11 as an illegal command and does not respond to it. The
dw_mmc host reports "Unexpected CMD11 timeout". The core treats the
failed voltage switch as missing UHS-I support and initializes the card
in high speed mode at 50 MHz.

There is nothing to switch when the host already signals at 1.8V. So
skip the voltage switch in that case, regardless of S18A. The following
CCCR read still decides which UHS-I modes the card supports. This is
the same path that a card takes which correctly reports S18A as 0.

Hosts with a switchable I/O supply power up at 3.3V and still send
CMD11. Hosts with a fixed 1.8V supply no longer send CMD11 to cards
that set S18A. Cards that set S18A at 1.8V and relied on the CMD11
handshake now have to run UHS-I from the CCCR without it. Tests on
such hosts are welcome.

With this change the RTL8822CS on the NanoPi R2S Plus initializes in
SDR104 mode at 150 MHz. The Wi-Fi throughput roughly doubles. The NanoPi
R76S and the LincStation E1 use the same module on a fixed 1.8V supply
with sd-uhs-sdr104 as well.

The analysis and the first draft of this patch and of its changelog
were produced in Claude Code sessions with the Claude model
claude-fable-5-1. The same sessions ran the tests on an RTL8822CS on
the sdmmc_ext controller of a NanoPi R2S Plus. The card then
initializes in SDR104 mode at 150 MHz without sending CMD11, and tuning
succeeds. In station mode the throughput rises from 30 to 61 Mbit/s for
transmit and from about 70 to 126 Mbit/s for receive. The author
reviewed the change and the results.

Signed-off-by: Grische <github@grische.xyz>
---
 drivers/mmc/core/sdio.c | 9 ++++++++-
 1 file changed, 8 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

--- a/drivers/mmc/core/sdio.c
+++ b/drivers/mmc/core/sdio.c
@@ -738,8 +738,15 @@
 	 * fails to check rocr & R4_18V_PRESENT,  but we still need to
 	 * try to init uhs card. sdio_read_cccr will take over this task
 	 * to make sure which speed mode should work.
+	 *
+	 * Some cards, like the Realtek RTL8822CS, set S18A even if the
+	 * signal voltage is already 1.8V. Per SD spec v3.01, section
+	 * 4.2.4.4, such a card treats CMD11 as an illegal command and does
+	 * not respond to it. There is nothing to switch in that case, so
+	 * skip the voltage switch if the host already signals at 1.8V.
 	 */
-	if (rocr & ocr & R4_18V_PRESENT) {
+	if (rocr & ocr & R4_18V_PRESENT &&
+	    host->ios.signal_voltage != MMC_SIGNAL_VOLTAGE_180) {
 		err = mmc_set_uhs_voltage(host, ocr_card);
 		if (err == -EAGAIN) {
 			mmc_sdio_pre_init(host, ocr_card, card);
